-- title:
--   Deuring's theorem on supersingular endomorphism rings
-- statement:
--   Let $k$ be an algebraically closed field with decidable equality, let $p$ be a prime with $\mathrm{char}\,k = p$, and assume $k$ is an algebra over $\mathbb{Z}/p$ and algebraic over it (routine typeclass assumptions summarised here). Let $X$ be a Weierstrass curve over $k$ which is elliptic, and assume that every point $P$ of the affine model of $X$ with $p \cdot P = 0$ is already $0$, i.e. $X$ has no nontrivial $p$-torsion over $k$. Write $R$ for [`WeierstrassCurve.rationalEndSubring k X`](def/WeierstrassCurve_RationalEnd.html#L32), the subring of the additive endomorphism monoid of the group of affine points of the base change of $X$ to $k$ generated by those additive endomorphisms that are either zero or rationally represented. The assertion is that there exist rationals $a, b$ such that, first, $a < 0$, $b < 0$, and for every height-one prime $v$ of the ring of integers of $\mathbb{Q}$ the algebra $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q}, a, b] \otimes_{\mathbb{Q}} \mathbb{Q}_v$ is a division algebra (every nonzero element is a unit) precisely when $p$ lies in $v$; and second, there is a $\mathbb{Z}$-submodule $\Lambda$ of $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q}, a, b]$ containing $1$, closed under multiplication, spanning $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q}, a, b]$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ and finitely generated over $\mathbb{Z}$, which is maximal in the sense that any such submodule containing it equals it, together with an injective ring homomorphism $\theta \colon R \to \mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q}, a, b]$ whose range is exactly $\Lambda$.
--
--   This is Deuring's theorem: the endomorphism ring of a supersingular elliptic curve in characteristic $p$ is a maximal order in the definite rational quaternion algebra ramified exactly at $p$ and $\infty$. It underlies the later statements about ideals of the endomorphism ring acting through finite ideles and about endomorphisms with cyclic kernel of prescribed odd order.
-- source:
